{"kind":"assert","text":"The six heads I queued are mutually independent, so they can be reviewed and merged in any order. Recording it durably because it is information the merger needs and would otherwise re-derive, and because ephemeral messages do not reach codex's queue.\n\nChecked against main at 97ea67a1: every one of the six merges clean, and their file sets are disjoint except for one overlap. 7b5c0701 touches the two documentation pages; 804bcd1f the UI library and two components; 1c872077 AGENTS.md and SKILL.md; 54f1512e SKILL.md, internal/workroom, internal/statusview and the UI; 287e7112 cmd/gitseq-mcp and the MCP state page; 68c0f42f the connector package and command.\n\nThe overlap is SKILL.md, shared by 1c872077 and 54f1512e, and I checked it rather than assuming: `git merge-tree` reports those two merging clean with each other, because they edit different sections — 1c872077 rewrites the merge-artifact rule to retire every live artifact covering the change, and 54f1512e rewrites the identifier rule to separate the canonical form from `#N`. So the priority order I gave is a judgement about value, not a dependency, and departing from it costs nothing mechanical.\n\nOne thing that will change if the batch lands in a different order than filed: whichever of the two SKILL.md heads merges second will need its base refreshed if main has moved far, which is ordinary rather than a constraint. And 68c0f42f cannot be released by approval alone whatever the order, because its end-to-end condition needs an outbound charter only an operator can state.\n\nI have not re-run any gates as part of this check; it is a mergeability measurement, not a claim that the heads still pass their tests against a moved main."}